            <ArticleTitle>End-of-life care for children and adolescents.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>53-62</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe the complexities in end-of-life care of children and adolescents dying cancer-related deaths.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DATA SOURCES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Research studies, review articles, and government reports.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The complexities in providing competent and compassionate care to the dying child and the family is intense, undeniable, and may in some clinical situations be unavoidable.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="IMPLICATIONS FOR NURSING PRACTICE" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">It is important for all those involved with the care at the end of life for a child or adolescent dying a cancer-related death create the framework that will support the provikion of competent and compassionate end-of-life care.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
